    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> sailed his fleet at the Franco-Spanish line in two columns, hoping to break it into pieces and force a chaotic mêlée in which his better-trained crews would prevail.</li><li>The British lost no ship; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> himself was shot by a French marksman and died about three hours later, shortly before the battle ended.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had already given up the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> before the battle, and Trafalgar had a limited effect on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-third-coalition">War of the Third Coalition</a>, but Britain's command of the sea was not tested for over a century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The British ships were painted in a yellow and black pattern, later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> Chequer, so that they would be easy to tell from their opponents.</p></aside>
    </section>

