    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">Early 1805</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> plans to take control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> for an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and Villeneuve evades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> and crosses the Atlantic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1805</span><span class="rf-text">Calder fights Villeneuve inconclusively at Cape Finisterre, and two Spanish ships are captured</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1805</span><span class="rf-text">Villeneuve sails south to Cádiz instead of north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a>, and the invasion force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> marches into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1805</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> takes command of the British fleet off Cádiz</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1805</span><span class="rf-text">Villeneuve orders the Combined Fleet to sail from Cádiz</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1805</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s two columns break the Franco-Spanish line off Cape Trafalgar, and Nelson is mortally wounded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1805</span><span class="rf-text">Commodore Cosmao leads a sortie from Cádiz and a storm wrecks many of the prizes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1805</span><span class="rf-text">Strachan's squadron captures the four ships that had fled under Dumanoir</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-trafalgar" data-v="62a13dd6e9" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The fleets close in a ragged line and two columns</b> The French and Spanish were in a ragged curved line headed north, with the coast behind them. The British came from the west in two columns: Nelson's to the north, Collingwood's to the south.</li><li><b>Nelson feints at the van and the columns converge</b> Nelson led his column in a feint toward the van and then turned toward the real point of attack, while Collingwood altered course so that the two columns converged. The foremost British ships came under heavy fire for almost an hour before their guns could bear.</li><li><b>Royal Sovereign breaks the line</b> Royal Sovereign outran the rest of the British fleet and came under fire from four allied ships. She then broke the line just astern of the Spanish flagship Santa Ana.</li><li><b>Victory cuts between Bucentaure and Redoutable</b> Nelson's flagship Victory cut the line between Villeneuve's flagship Bucentaure and Redoutable. Her broadside through Bucentaure's stern knocked the French flagship out of action, and a general mêlée followed.</li><li><b>Victory locks masts with Redoutable</b> Victory engaged the 74-gun Redoutable and locked masts with her. Redoutable's crew, with a strong infantry corps, gathered to board Victory, and Nelson was shot from her mizzentop.</li><li><b>Temeraire fires on the French crew</b> As the French prepared to board Victory, Temeraire, second ship of the windward column, came up on the starboard bow of Redoutable. She fired a carronade into the exposed French crew and caused many casualties.</li><li><b>Redoutable surrenders</b> Captain Lucas of Redoutable, severely wounded and with 99 fit men left out of 643, surrendered. Bucentaure was isolated by Victory and Temeraire and then engaged by three more British ships.</li><li><b>The allied centre and rear are overwhelmed</b> More British ships entered the battle and the allied centre and rear were gradually overwhelmed. The British ships of both columns stayed engaged with them.</li><li><b>Dumanoir breaks off with four ships</b> Dumanoir tried to help the collapsing centre. After failing to fight his way through, he led four French ships away from the fighting. The British took 20 allied vessels and lost none.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1805 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">First French Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> was the dominant military land power on the European continent, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> prevailed at sea through disruptive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockades</a>. When the Third Coalition declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Peace of Amiens</a>, Napoleon renewed his determination to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. To bring his invasion flotilla to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> he needed to wrest control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> from the Royal Navy. His plan was for the French and Spanish fleets in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and at Cádiz to break through the blockade and join forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, then return, help the fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a> to emerge, and together clear the Channel.</p><p>Early in 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> blockaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> loosely, hoping to lure the French out. Villeneuve evaded him when storms blew the British off station, passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a>, joined the Spanish fleet at Cádiz and sailed for the Caribbean. Nelson pursued and missed him by just days in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> as a result of false information. Villeneuve returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Calder fought him inconclusively at Cape Finisterre on 22 July. Ordered to Brest, Villeneuve instead sailed south to Cádiz on 11 August. On 25 August the invasion force near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> broke camp and marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which ended the immediate threat of invasion.</p><p>Nelson, who had returned home, sailed in HMS Victory on 15 September and took command off Cádiz on 28 September. On 16 September Napoleon ordered the ships at Cádiz to put to sea, land soldiers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and fight if they met a numerically inferior British fleet.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Trafalgar&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Cádiz, Supply situation, Allied collapse:</p><ul><li>Nicholas Best, Trafalgar (Weidenfeld &amp; Nicolson, 2005)</li><li>Alexander Stilwell (ed.), The Trafalgar Companion (Osprey Publishing, 2005)</li><li>Gregory Fremont-Barnes, Trafalgar 1805: Nelson's Crowning Victory (Osprey Publishing, 2005)</li><li>Tom Pocock, Trafalgar: an eyewitness history (Penguin Classics, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson.jpg" alt="Horatio Nelson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HoratioNelson1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lemuel Francis Abbott, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 21 October <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> had 27 ships of the line with 2,148 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and a total of 17,000 crewmen and marines. His flagship Victory, captained by Thomas Masterman Hardy, was one of three 100-gun first-rates; he also had four 98-gun second-rates and 20 third-rates, three of them 64-gun ships then being phased out. There were also four frigates, a schooner and a cutter. The second in command, Vice-Admiral Cuthbert Collingwood, led one column in the 100-gun Royal Sovereign.</p><p>Villeneuve, flying his flag in Bucentaure, had 33 ships of the line, among them some of the largest in the world: the Spanish Santísima Trinidad of 130 guns and the 112-gun Príncipe de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturias</a> and Santa Ana. The Spanish contributed 15 ships of the line and the French 18, with some 30,000 men and 2,632 cannons; another count gives nearly 30,000 men and 2,568 guns. The fleet also had five frigates and two brigs, all French. Its crews included few experienced sailors, ships had been kept in harbour for years by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, and gunnery was neglected. Villeneuve's ships were also more than two thousand men short of the force needed to sail.</p><p>Nelson meant to cut the opposing line in three, approaching in two columns at nearly a right angle, one towards the centre and one towards the rear, and to force a mêlée. He told his captains that no captain could do very wrong who placed his ship alongside an enemy's. Villeneuve guessed that Nelson would drive at his line, but, fearing that his inexperienced officers could not keep formation in more than one group, kept a single line.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Trafalgar&#8221; cites where it covers Nelson's plan:</p><ul><li>Nicholas Best, Trafalgar (Weidenfeld &amp; Nicolson, 2005)</li><li>Colin White, Nelson the Admiral (Sutton Publishing Limited, 2005)</li><li>Gregory Fremont-Barnes, The Royal Navy, 1793–1815 (Osprey Publishing, 2007)</li><li>Alexander Stilwell (ed.), The Trafalgar Companion (Osprey Publishing, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 21 October the Franco-Spanish fleet formed a sprawling, uneven line. At 8 a.m. Villeneuve ordered it to turn about and return to Cádiz, and in the light, shifting wind this took nearly an hour and a half, leaving an uneven crescent drawn out nearly 5 miles (8 km) long. At 11:45 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> signalled "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> expects that every man will do his duty". Nelson in Victory led the northern column and Collingwood in Royal Sovereign the leeward one. As the wind was very light, the leading British ships were under heavy fire from several allied ships for almost an hour before their own guns could bear.</p><p>At noon Villeneuve signalled "engage the enemy" and Fougueux fired her first shot at Royal Sovereign, which, her bottom recently cleaned, outran the rest and broke the line just astern of Santa Ana with a double-shotted raking broadside. At 12:45 Victory cut the line between Bucentaure and Redoutable and fired a treble-shotted raking broadside through Bucentaure's stern, which killed and wounded some 200 to 400 of its 800 men and dismasted it. Victory then locked masts with Redoutable, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> bullet fired from Redoutable's mizzentop struck Nelson in the left shoulder and passed through his spine.</p><p>Temeraire fired a carronade on Redoutable's exposed crew; at 13:55 Captain Lucas, with 99 fit men out of 643, surrendered. Santísima Trinidad surrendered after three hours. The allied centre and rear were gradually overwhelmed. Gravina was wounded, and Churruca and Alcalá-Galiano were killed after ordering their ships not to surrender. Nelson died at half-past four, three hours after being hit.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Trafalgar&#8221; cites where it covers Main engagement:</p><ul><li>Sir Julian Stafford Corbett, The campaign of Trafalgar (Longmans, Green, and company, 1919)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The British took 20 vessels of the Franco-Spanish fleet and lost none. Redoutable sank, Santísima Trinidad and Argonauta were scuttled by the British, Achille exploded, Intrépide and San Agustín burned, and Aigle, Berwick, Fougueux and Monarca were wrecked in the gale that followed. Dumanoir led four ships away from the fighting; they were captured by Strachan's squadron on 4 November at the Battle of Cape Ortegal. Only eleven allied ships escaped to Cádiz and only five were seaworthy. Gravina passed command to Commodore Cosmao on 23 October and died months later of his wounds.</p><p>Cosmao sailed out with ships and frigates to try to recapture prizes, and French frigates retook two Spanish ships, Santa Ana and Neptuno, whose crews rose against their British prize crews. In the heavy seas Collingwood ordered his ships to cast off their prizes, and the allied squadron lost one French and two Spanish vessels. Indomptable was wrecked with 1,200 men on board, of whom no more than 100 were saved. The British burnt or sank four more prizes, and only four reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. On 27 October he offered to set Spanish wounded ashore, and the Spanish governor and Gravina released British prisoners in exchange.</p><p>Villeneuve was captured with his flagship and taken to Britain. After his parole in 1806 he was found dead in an inn on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> with six stab wounds, officially recorded as suicide. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s body, kept in a barrel of brandy, went home for a state funeral. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> kept the defeat secret for over a month.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known through those who were there. The signal made at 11:45 comes from Lieutenant Pasco, who recalled that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> first wished to say "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> confides" and agreed to "expects" because that word was in the signal vocabulary. Surgeon William Beatty heard Nelson murmur "Thank God I have done my duty", while his chaplain, Alexander Scott, recorded the last words as "God and my country". The Nelson historian Craig Cabell has suggested that Nelson was reciting his own prayer, in which the words "God" and "my country" are closely linked. Collingwood's letter to the Admiralty of November 1805 describes the storm.</p><p>Accounts of the allied sortie differ. Cosmao claimed in his report that the rescue plan was entirely his idea, but Vice-Admiral Escaño recorded a meeting of Spanish and French commodores at which it was discussed and agreed. Counts of the allied guns differ, 2,632 or 2,568, and the allied ships lost are counted as 18 captured or destroyed, or as 20 taken.</p><p>The news was controlled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> did not hear of the battle for weeks and kept the defeat a closely guarded secret for over a month, and a fabricated text declaring a spectacular victory for the French and Spanish was published and attributed to Le Moniteur Universel. Historians also differ on why the losses were so uneven. Some attribute the disparity less to Nelson's tactics than to the fighting readiness of the fleets, though Villeneuve's fleet had just spent months at sea, which supports the view that the leaders' morale was the main difference.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars.jpg" alt="Napoleonic Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoleonic_Wars_(revision).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruedi33a, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The victory confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s naval supremacy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had already abandoned his invasion plans before the battle and never revived them, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was never again seriously challenged by the French fleet in a large-scale engagement. The battle did not end the French naval challenge: Britain acted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1807 and elsewhere in 1808 to stop smaller navies falling into French hands, and Napoleon's shipbuilding programme had produced a fleet of 80 ships of the line by 1814. For almost ten years the Royal Navy maintained a close <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of French bases. Trafalgar had a negligible impact on the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-third-coalition">War of the Third Coalition</a>: less than two months later Napoleon defeated the Coalition at Austerlitz, and the Napoleonic Wars continued for another ten years.</p><p>The Royal Navy's dominance was not meaningfully tested until Jutland in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Modern analyses suggest that relative economic strength was an important underlying cause of British naval mastery, though Trafalgar was given as the reason at the time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s unorthodox tactics were seldom emulated by later generations.</p><p>Nelson remains Britain's greatest naval war hero. Monuments to him include the one on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> Green of 1806, Nelson's Pillar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, erected in 1808 and destroyed by a bombing in 1966, Nelson's Monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, and Nelson's Column in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>'s Trafalgar Square is named for the battle, and Nelson's Column there was finished in 1843. In 2005 a Fleet Review in the Solent had 167 ships from 35 nations.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Trafalgar on 21 October 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s fleet broke the line of a larger Franco-Spanish fleet under Villeneuve and took 20 ships without losing one, though Nelson was killed. It ended any real French effort to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s command of the sea untested for over a century.</em></p>

